  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: ba718376424a4d74abfc4131416ba2fa, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 1510115985891328
    m_Localized: "Cesare Lombroso (1835\u20131909) was an Italian physician and criminologist
      whose work profoundly influenced the early development of forensic science.
      A pioneering\u2014and controversial\u2014figure, Lombroso attempted to explain
      criminal behavior through biological and anthropological study, proposing theories
      that today are recognized as scientifically flawed and shaped by the cultural
      beliefs of his time.\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510280289361920
    m_Localized: The museum
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510306809946112
    m_Localized: "The criminal anthropology museum hosts his laboratory collections,
      research materials, and personal archives, allowing visitors to explore the
      origins of modern criminology while critically examining the assumptions and
      biases that guided 19th-century scientific thought. Through this historical
      lens, the museum invites reflection on how ideas about crime, identity, and
      society have evolved\u2014and how they continue to shape contemporary debates."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510330079944704
    m_Localized: Pseudoscience and controversy
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510366939488256
    m_Localized: "The theories developed by Cesare Lombroso are today widely recognized
      as forms of pseudoscience grounded in 19th-century assumptions about biology,
      race, class, and social \u201Cdeviance.\u201D His work often reinforced racist,
      classist, and ableist stereotypes, and contributed to discriminatory practices
      that targeted marginalized communities. The Lombroso Museum does not endorse
      these ideas. Instead, it presents them within their historical context to encourage
      critical reflection on how scientific authority can be misused and how such
      narratives have shaped social policy, criminal justice, and public perceptions
      of difference. Visitors are invited to approach the collections with awareness
      of these ethical and historical issues and to engage thoughtfully with the
      complex legacy of Lombroso\u2019s work."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510424854437888
    m_Localized: Tickets
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510449139458048
    m_Localized: "Monday to Saturday, 10:00\u201318:00 (last admission at 17:30).\nClosed
      on Sundays."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510561253203968
    m_Localized: "   - <b>Full price:</b> \u20AC5.00\n\n   - <b>Reduced:</b> \u20AC3.00"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1510829806100480
    m_Localized: Free entry every Wednesday throughout the year, and for holders
      of the <i>Abbonamento Musei Torino Piemonte</i> and the <i>Torino+Piemonte
      Card</i>.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511004293341184
    m_Localized: Reduced and free admissions
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511031820558336
    m_Localized: 'Who qualifies for reduced admission:'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511066113187840
    m_Localized: "Visitors over 65; visitors aged 10\u201318; university students
      (excluding the University of Turin) with valid student ID; members of ANMS
      and FAI; and groups with a reservation."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511084433907712
    m_Localized: 'Who qualifies for free admission:'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511102742044672
    m_Localized: Children under 10; students and staff of the University of Turin;
      ICOM members; directors and curators of Italian and foreign museums; school
      classes from public institutions of all levels with advance booking; visitors
      with disabilities and their companions; officially licensed tour guides; personnel
      of the Italian Ministry of Culture in managerial roles; registered journalists;
      and current municipal, district, and regional administrators of Turin and Piedmont,
      as well as staff of the Experimental Institute for Plant Nutrition.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511122329444352
    m_Localized: Guided Tours
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511136397139968
    m_Localized: "\u20AC35.00 (1 hour)."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511183239127040
    m_Localized: Educational services
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511206299410432
    m_Localized: The museum offers educational activities for students of all ages
      from primary school to college, promoting scientific and cultural learning
      through their unique collections. Exhibits cover topics such as human anatomy,
      criminology, biodiversity, world cultures, and the history of science. All
      activities use a hands-on, investigative approach designed to spark curiosity,
      support scientific thinking, and build key learning skills.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511248955482112
    m_Localized: Elementary
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511270891692032
    m_Localized: "School Year 2025\u201326"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511319684030464
    m_Localized: 'Our activities for primary schools are designed as hands-on experiences
      that connect classroom learning with direct observation and guided scientific
      storytelling. Students are encouraged to explore, compare past and present,
      and develop curiosity and critical thinking.

      Since the contents of the
      museum may disturb young children, the activities are available for <b>grades
      4-5 only</b>'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511400323719168
    m_Localized: Guided tours
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511418870931456
    m_Localized: "A 60-minute tour designed to encourage careful observation and
      critical thinking. Each student receives a free educational booklet for use
      during the visit and afterward in class.\nCost: \u20AC35 per class (max 25
      students)"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511433576161280
    m_Localized: Educational Workshops
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511475179462656
    m_Localized: "<b>From Sherlock Holmes to Fingerprints</b>\nA 90-minute hands-on
      workshop exploring investigative methods, experimentation, and the basics of
      forensic observation.\nCost: \u20AC75 per class (max 25 students)\n\nMuseum
      admission is free for all school groups.\nBooking is required for both tours
      and workshops:\nCall 011 6707732 (Mon\u2013Fri, 9:00\u201313:00 / 14:00\u201315:00)
      or email <i>didattica.musei@unito.it</i>\n\nActivities available from 10 September
      2025."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511645451427840
    m_Localized: Middle schools
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511680289316864
    m_Localized: "School Year 2025\u201326"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511694713528320
    m_Localized: Visits for lower secondary school classes offer an immersive experience
      that combines direct observation of collections with guided analysis and critical
      reflection. Students explore themes such as scientific method, historical context,
      and the relationship between science and society.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511709863354368
    m_Localized: Guided tours
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511732172857344
    m_Localized: "A 60-minute tour designed to encourage active questioning, comparison
      of sources, and an interdisciplinary understanding of the museum\u2019s themes.\nCost:
      \u20AC35 per class (max 25 students)"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511746253135872
    m_Localized: Educational Workshops
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511789450272768
    m_Localized: "<b>From Sherlock Holmes to Fingerprints</b>\nA 90-minute hands-on
      workshop exploring investigative methods, experimentation, and the basics of
      forensic observation.\nCost: \u20AC75 per class (max 25 students)\n\nMuseum
      admission is free for all school groups.\nBooking is required for both tours
      and workshops:\nCall 011 6707732 (Mon\u2013Fri, 9:00\u201313:00 / 14:00\u201315:00)
      or email <i>didattica.musei@unito.it</i>\n\nActivities available from 10 September
      2025."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511963157372928
    m_Localized: High schools
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1511998339194880
    m_Localized: "School Year 2025\u201326"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512014537596928
    m_Localized: "Activities for upper secondary school classes are designed as in-depth,
      critical explorations of the relationship between science, history, society,
      and identity within the museum\u2019s collections."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512032136896512
    m_Localized: Guided tours
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512102907387904
    m_Localized: "A 60-minute visit offering a deeper analysis of historical sources
      and museum objects, encouraging critical thinking and connections to civic
      education and the UN 2030 Agenda.\nCost: \u20AC35 per class (max 25 students)"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512124071845888
    m_Localized: Educational Workshops
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512151263518720
    m_Localized: "<b>From Sherlock Holmes to Fingerprints</b>\nA 90-minute participatory
      workshop that promotes active learning through hands-on investigation and scientific
      reasoning.\nCost: \u20AC75 per class (max 25 students)\n\nMuseum admission
      is free for all school groups.\nBooking is required for both tours and workshops:\nCall
      011 6707732 (Mon\u2013Fri, 9:00\u201313:00 / 14:00\u201315:00) or email <i>didattica.musei@unito.it</i>\n\nActivities
      available from 10 September 2025."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512241482997760
    m_Localized: Location
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512263553425408
    m_Localized: "<b>Via Pietro Giuria 15 \u2013 10126 Turin</b>\nTel. +39 011 6708195
      \u2013 Fax +39 011 6708196\nEmail: museo.lombroso@unito.it"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512343647854592
    m_Localized: Historical archive
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512370160050176
    m_Localized: "The University of Turin preserves a vast archival heritage, with
      inventories progressively made available online in accordance with international
      standards. The Historical Archive gathers documents produced by the University\u2019s
      administrative offices, while records created by certain scientific institutes
      are kept within the institutions that inherited them.\n\nThe Museum\u2019s
      Archive is available online on the museum's website.\nTo consult materials
      in person, a reservation is required via email at museo.lombroso@unito.it\n\nMore
      than 2,600 letters written or received by Cesare Lombroso are accessible online
      through the #LombrosoProject portal. The collection includes around 800 outgoing
      letters and about 1,800 incoming letters, allowing for the virtual reconstruction
      of Lombroso\u2019s surviving correspondence.\n(The project was carried out
      by the University Museum System with the support of Fondazione CRT and the
      Department of Historical Studies of the University of Turin.)"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512388019396608
    m_Localized: 'Museum''s history

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1512421288615936
    m_Localized: "Cesare Lombroso began collecting anatomical and criminal anthropology
      specimens in 1859. The collection moved several times\u2014Via della Zecca,
      Via Po, Via Michelangelo, and Corso Galileo Galilei\u2014before being partially
      displayed in the 1985 exhibition La scienza e la colpa. In 2001, it found a
      permanent home at Via Giuria 15, within the Palazzo degli Istituti Anatomici,
      alongside the Museums of Human Anatomy, Anthropology & Ethnography, and, since
      2006, the Museum of Fruit, forming a center dedicated to Turin\u2019s scientific
      positivism of the 19th and early 20th centuries."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1810668506882048
    m_Localized: The first room welcomes the visitors with an overview of Lombroso's
      statistical studies throughout Italy of the time.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1811814667558912
    m_Localized: "<b>Carta geografica dell\u2019Italia con distribuzione di dati
      sul \u201Cdifetto di statura\u201D tra i militari (1863-1876).</b>\n\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1917459513253888
    m_Localized: '<b>Portrait of Cesare Lombroso</b>

      This portrait depicts
      Cesare Lombroso, the 19th-century physician whose work shaped the early development
      of criminal anthropology. Often shown surrounded by books or scientific instruments,
      Lombroso is presented here as a researcher deeply engaged in his studies. Today,
      his image invites visitors to reflect not only on his historical influence
      but also on the controversies and biases that marked his theories.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1918027161968640
    m_Localized: The room focuses on some of the instruments used by Lombroso to
      study the criminals' bodies as he looked for a correlation between anatomy
      and behaviour.
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1919163952570368
    m_Localized: "<b>19th-Century Pendulum Clock</b>\nThis small pendulum clock is
      a simple example of the domestic technologies available in the late 1800s.
      Though not directly linked to Lombroso\u2019s research, it reflects the everyday
      material culture of the period in which his work developed, as long as the
      kind of technologies that were available at the time."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1921569222336512
    m_Localized: "<b>Anfossi\u2019s Craniographer</b>\nDesigned in the late 19th
      century by Italian physician Carlo Anfossi, this instrument was used to take
      precise measurements of the skull. Tools like the craniographer played a key
      role in anthropometric practices of the time, including those later adopted
      by Lombroso. Today, such devices illustrate how 19th-century science attempted
      to classify human diversity through physical measurements\u2014an approach
      now recognized as scientifically unfounded."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1922294769819648
    m_Localized: "<b>Antique Camera</b>\nThis early photographic camera represents
      the technology used in the late 19th and early 20th centuries to document people,
      objects, and scientific specimens. Photography played an important role in
      Lombroso\u2019s work, providing visual records he used to support his theories.
      Today, such equipment highlights the growing reliance on images in scientific
      practice, as well as the ways photographs were used to construct\u2014and sometimes
      distort\u2014ideas about identity and criminality."
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
